{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggwYchUjHFLoiuYsLDKh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Answer:** Dq = 2660 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 529 cm⁻¹, C ≈ 2856 cm⁻¹ (C/B ≈ 5.4).\n\n**1. Dq from the ⁴T₂ band**\n\nFor an octahedral d³ ion, the ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂ band sits at exactly 10Dq.\nSo 10Dq = 26 600 cm⁻¹, which gives **Dq = 2660 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**2. B from the ⁴T₁(F) band**\n\nWrite Δ = 10Dq. The ⁴T₁(F) energy is:\nν₂ = 7.5B + 1.5Δ − ½√(225B² − 18BΔ + Δ²)\n\nPut in ν₂ = 32 500 and Δ = 26 600:\n- Rearranging gives √(225B² − 478 800B + 7.0756×10⁸) = 14 800 + 15B.\n- Squaring both sides, the B² terms cancel: 4.8852×10⁸ = 922 800 B.\n- So **B ≈ 529 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**3. C from the ²E band**\n\nTo first order, the ²E energy is about 9B + 3C − 90B²/Δ. Setting this equal to 12 820 cm⁻¹ gives C ≈ 3000 cm⁻¹ as a starting guess.\n\nI then used the tool to compute the full d³ energy matrices at Dq = 2660 and B = 529.4:\n- C = 3001 puts ²E at 13 198 cm⁻¹, too high.\n- C = 2866 puts ²E at 12 790 cm⁻¹, slightly too low.\n- The ²E energy changes by about 3.0 cm⁻¹ for each 1 cm⁻¹ change in C. Interpolating, ²E = 12 820 cm⁻¹ needs **C ≈ 2856 cm⁻¹**. This value is interpolated, not computed directly.\n\nAt C = 2866 the computed ⁴T₂ (26 600 cm⁻¹) and ⁴T₁ (32 500 cm⁻¹) bands match the data exactly. Neither depends on C, so they still match at C ≈ 2856.\n\n**Comparison with the free ion**\n\nFree Cr³⁺ has B of about 918 cm⁻¹. The fitted B is about 58% of that (β ≈ 0.58). This large drop reflects the strongly covalent Cr–CN bonding expected for cyanide.\n\n{\"Dq\": 2660, \"B\": 529, \"C\": 2856, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq from 4T2, B from the 4T1(F) band, C fitted to the 2E band using the full d3 energy matrices.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2187,"cache_read_input_tokens":10859,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":218